\subsection{Spectral Metrics}
\label{subsec:spectral_metrics}

Given a language model at generation $t$, we extract last-layer embeddings for $n{=}1{,}000$ held-out samples to form $\mathbf{E} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d}$ and compute the Gram matrix $\mathbf{G} = \mathbf{E}^\top \mathbf{E}$.
Let $\sigma_1 \geq \sigma_2 \geq \cdots \geq \sigma_d$ denote the singular values of $\mathbf{E}$.
We track three complementary spectral indicators:

\paragraph{Spectral Log-Volume (SLV).}
$\text{SLV} = \sum_i \log \sigma_i = \frac{1}{2} \log \det \mathbf{G}$.
This quantity measures the total volume spanned by the embedding cloud in log-space.
Because it sums over all singular values, SLV is sensitive to contraction along any spectral direction, not only the dominant ones.

\paragraph{Effective Rank.}
$r_{\text{eff}} = \exp(H(\mathbf{p}))$, where $p_i = \sigma_i / \sum_j \sigma_j$ and $H$ is the Shannon entropy, measuring how many dimensions carry substantial variance (uniform $\to d$, concentrated $\to 1$).

\paragraph{Leading Singular Value.}
$\sigma_1$ tracks the dominant spectral direction; rising $\sigma_1$ under falling $r_{\text{eff}}$ signals representational collapse into a low-dimensional subspace.

\subsection{Adaptive Mixing Controller}
\label{subsec:controller}

We propose a closed-loop controller that adjusts the real-data mixing ratio $r_t$ at each generation based on the rate of spectral change.
The update rule is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:controller}
r_{t+1} = \frac{B_{\text{rem}}}{T - t} + k \cdot \max(0,\; \Delta_t - \tau)
\end{equation}
where $B_{\text{rem}}$ is the remaining real-data budget (fraction of total budget not yet consumed), $T$ is the total number of generations, $\tau{=}0.002$ is a sensitivity threshold, and $k{=}10$ is the controller gain.
The signal $\Delta_t$ is the baseline-normalized SLV drop: $\Delta_t = (\widetilde{S}_{t-1} - \widetilde{S}_t) / |\widetilde{S}_0|$, where $\widetilde{S}_t$ is the exponential moving average (EMA)-smoothed SLV ($\alpha{=}0.5$) and $\widetilde{S}_0$ is initialized from the pretrained model (Gen~0, SLV${=}2366.68$).
The signed form ensures that spectral decompression ($\Delta_t < 0$) does not trigger the controller; only contraction activates the $\max(0, \cdot)$ gate in Eq.~\ref{eq:controller}.
The output is clipped to $[r_{\min}, r_{\max}] = [0.20, 0.80]$ and subject to the global constraint $\sum_t r_t \leq B_{\text{total}}$.

The first term distributes the remaining budget uniformly across future generations; the model degrades gracefully even without spectral signal.
The second term injects additional real data when SLV contraction exceeds the threshold $\tau$, proportional to the severity of the spectral drop.
This design requires no lookahead and operates with a single hyperparameter choice ($k$) beyond the budget constraint.

\paragraph{Controller Silence Condition.}
Define $r_{\text{crit}}$ as the minimum constant mixing ratio such that SLV is non-decreasing from the second generation onward: $\widetilde{S}_t \geq \widetilde{S}_{t-1}$ for all $t \in \{2, \ldots, T\}$ (excluding the initial fine-tuning transient at $t{=}1$).
When a constant ratio $r \geq r_{\text{crit}}$ is applied, $\Delta_t \leq 0 < \tau$ for all $t \geq 2$ and any $\tau > 0$, so the controller boost is identically zero.
This $\tau$-independence means the paradox cannot be resolved by lowering the sensitivity threshold; the same argument extends to cumulative variants.
Our experiments (\S\ref{subsec:phase1}) empirically bound $0.25 < r_{\text{crit}} \leq 0.3$ over Gen~2--10 for Pythia-410M on OpenWebText.

\subsection{Second-Order Spectral Indicator}
\label{subsec:second_order}

The signal attenuation paradox (\S\ref{subsec:controller}) arises because the first-order signal $\Delta_t$ becomes non-positive under sufficient real-data mixing.
We define a second-order indicator based on the rate of change of the SLV growth:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:second_order}
\Delta^2_t = \widetilde{\Delta}_{t-1} - \widetilde{\Delta}_t
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\Delta}_t$ is the EMA-smoothed first-order signal with smoothing parameter $\alpha_2$ (a tunable hyperparameter; we use $\alpha_2{=}0.3$ in our experiments).
Even when SLV rises monotonically ($\Delta_t < 0$ for all $t \geq 2$), a sustained negative $\Delta^2_t$ suggests that the growth rate is generally decelerating, a potential precursor to eventual stagnation or reversal.
Whether this deceleration signal generalizes across mixing ratios remains an open question (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}).

\subsection{Collapse Phase Diagram}
\label{subsec:phase_diagram}

We define the \emph{collapse phase diagram} as the mapping from constant mixing ratio $r$ to the SLV trajectory over $T$ generations.
For each $r$, we classify the outcome: \emph{collapse} if SLV declines monotonically after the decompression phase (Gen~2 onward), \emph{stable} if SLV is non-decreasing, and \emph{transitional} if SLV shows non-monotonic behavior after Gen~2 (both increases and decreases within the observation window).
The critical mixing ratio $r_{\text{crit}}$ (\S\ref{subsec:controller}) is the boundary between collapse and stability in this diagram.
We estimate $r_{\text{crit}}$ by evaluating multiple constant ratios and identifying the lowest $r$ for which $\widetilde{S}_t \geq \widetilde{S}_{t-1}$ holds for all $t \in \{2, \ldots, T\}$.

